cture — monitoring systems, safety layers, deployment review protocols — alongside the model weights. Raw model weights without safety tooling are more dangerous than no access at all. Handing a military operator an unaligned frontier model is like handing someone a nuclear reactor core without the containment vessel.\n\nThe talent dimension sharpens this further. The 300 to 500 senior AI safety researchers in the United States are not interchangeable engineers. They are the specific people who understand how to build alignment safeguards for frontier systems. When a supply chain risk designation forces their employer to choose between safety commitments and defense revenue, these researchers leave. They do not move to a compliant defense contractor. They leave the defense-adjacent ecosystem entirely — to academia, to international institutions, to early retirement. The designation does not just lose headcount. It eliminates the institutional knowledge required to make any military AI deployment safe. A military that destroys its own safety engineering capacity is not stronger. It is a military that will deploy systems it cannot control, against adversaries and eventually against its own population, with no one left who knows how to build the off switch.\n\nThe bright line is this. Genuine declared-war emergencies with full congressional authorization may justify time-limited, oversight-gated access to escrowed capabilities — under statutory conditions, with independent veto authority, with sunset enforcement. Peacetime procurement designations that coerce safety erosion are categorically illegitimate. They weaponize supply chain authority for a purpose — destroying safety infrastructure — that contradicts the statutory purpose of supply chain risk management, which is to secure the supply chain. The designation does not resolve the tension between military access and safety alignment. It detonates the safety ecosystem and leaves the military with capable systems and no one who knows how to keep them from turning on their operators.",
