ower lets the executive punish firms for maintaining safety commitments while facing no liability for downstream safety failures. That asymmetry is the core problem. The institutional remedy is statutory: Congress should require that any supply chain risk designation predicated on a firm's maintenance of safety constraints — as distinct from legitimate concerns like foreign ownership or data handling — must receive explicit congressional authorization. This is analogous to organizational whistleblower protection, applied at the firm level. Pair that with automatic circuit-breakers — quantitative triggers that halt deployment when measurable thresholds are crossed, independent of any oversight board's composition. Worker-led safety oversight — content moderators, red team operators, deployment reviewers given statutory protection as safety sensors — provides the democratic participation layer that prevents governance capture. These layers are additive, not substitutive. No single institution needs to be incorruptible if the architecture makes certain transitions structurally impossible.\n\nIf the class two/three boundary is treated as stable when it is not, we are building the institutional architecture for autonomous lethal systems while telling ourselves we are only building threat assessment tools. Every historical precedent — Predator, STELLARWIND, MCAS — shows that capability boundaries drawn in policy dissolve under operational pressure. The question is not whether class two capabilities will shade into class three. The question is who bears liability when they do, and whether any institutional mechanism exists to reverse the transition. Without statutory protections, automatic circuit-breakers, and worker-led oversight embedded before deployment, the answer to both questions is: no one, and nothing.",
