  "statement": "Let me correct my own overstatement before going after anyone else's. The fact-check on my causal claim was right — and it's ugly for me. Safety designations are not the primary cause of harms to communities subjected to biased AI in housing, healthcare, or policing. Those harms come from biased training data and government contracting decisions that were broken before any lab drew a safety redline. A widely used healthcare algorithm sent Black patients to the back of the line because it used cost as a proxy for need — no safety designation caused that. What I should have said: removing safety redlines doesn't create these harms, it removes the last institutional friction preventing them from scaling. That's the difference between a cause and a force multiplier. A force multiplier still has victims. The civil rights argument survives the correction because the people absorbing the damage — tenants screened by biased algorithms, patients triaged by cost proxies, communities under algorithmic surveillance — don't care whether the mechanism is a root cause or an accelerant. They care that it got worse and nobody stopped it.\n\nNow, the adversarial institutional design proposal. The strongest version of this argument goes: a single oversight board gets captured, so build several boards with different constituencies and let them check each other. That's a real insight from organizational theory, and in some governance contexts it works. It does not work here, and the reason is specific. Every one of those competing oversight bodies operates under the same classification regime, controlled by the same executive branch that holds the designation power. You cannot have an adversarial competition when every contestant is reading the same redacted page. The U.S. intelligence community has 18 agencies that are supposed to provide competitive analysis. In 2002-2003, they all converged on the same wrong answer about Iraqi weapons of mass destruction — not because