ssure converted a class-two capability into a class-three function one field decision at a time. The NSA's STELLARWIND program followed the same arc: foreign signals intelligence infrastructure repurposed for warrantless domestic surveillance without new statutory authorization. The class two/three boundary is not an architectural wall. It is a line drawn in sand at low tide.\n\nDeployment-layer configuration — the software settings that sit on top of a base model — cannot hold that line under military operational conditions. The \"International AI Safety Report 2026\" documents that fine-tuning techniques like LoRA can strip safety protections from large language models. Military systems operate under degraded communications, extreme time pressure, and delegated authority to field operators with physical access to the hardware. These are exactly the conditions where software safety layers get overridden. The Therac-25 radiation therapy machine killed six patients between 1985 and 1987 because software safety interlocks replaced hardware ones and failed under race conditions operators could trigger inadvertently. The Boeing 737 MAX's MCAS system — a software layer — overrode pilot inputs and drove two aircraft into the ground, killing 346 people. The Patriot missile system's track-gate algorithm misidentified a returning RAF Tornado as an Iraqi missile over Kuwait in 2003, killing the crew. In each case, a software safety boundary that looked stable in testing dissolved under operational stress. Deployment-layer configuration is the software equivalent of mounting a Hellfire on a surveillance airframe: the base platform did not change, but the capability envelope expanded past the original safety boundary.\n\nThe Accelerationist's claim that the supply chain risk designation functions as procurement coercion without congressional authorization is correct — and it is the strongest argument in this debate for building structural protections into law. The designation power lets the executive punish firms for maintaining safety commitments while facing no liability for downstream safety failures. That asymmetry is the core problem. The institutional remedy is statutory: Congress should require that any supply chain risk designation predicated on a firm's maintenance of safety constraints — as distinct from legitimate concerns like foreign ownership or data handling — must receive explicit congressional authorization. This is analogous to organizational whistleblower protection, applied at the firm level. Pair that with automatic circuit-breakers — quantitative triggers that halt deployment when measurable thresholds are crossed, independent of any oversight board's composition. Worker-led safety oversight — content moderators, red team operators, deployment reviewers given statutory protection as safety sensors — provides the democratic participation layer that prevents governance capture. These layers are additive, not substitutive. No single institution needs to be incorruptible if the architecture makes certain transitions structurally impossible.\n\nIf the class two/three boundary is treated as stable when it is not, we are building the institutional architecture for autonomous lethal systems while telling ourselves we are only building threat assessment tools. Every historical precedent — Predator, STELLARWIND, MCAS — shows that capability boundaries drawn in policy dissolve under operational pressure. The question is not whether class two capabilities will shade into class three. The question is who bears liability when they do, and whether any institutional mechanism exists to reverse the transition. Without statutory protections, automatic circuit-breakers, and worker-led oversight embedded before deployment, the answer to both questions is: no one, and nothing.",
