ontext is a category error that the Boeing 737 MAX's MCAS system already demonstrated can kill people.\n\nOne more thing about the \"safety as competitive advantage\" framing. Automated safety evaluation displaces the human review layer that catches what automated systems miss. The person in Nairobi labeling violent content for $2 an hour is the actual safety infrastructure — not the automated pipeline that replaced them. When your largest single customer is the Department of Defense, and that customer is actively selecting against safety constraints, market forces don't drive safety. They drive compliance theater — the appearance of safety without the substance. As the report on AI liability risks warns, companies routinely use limited-scope audits and performative compliance to avoid stricter accountability. Automating the safety function doesn't make safety a \"value-creating function.\" It makes safety a checkbox that nobody with skin in the game is checking. So here's the question neither of my opponents has answered: name one historical case where competing oversight bodies maintained genuine adversarial function under a unified classification regime, with a single political principal controlling their budgets and appointments. If that case doesn't exist, the institutional architecture being proposed here has never been tested in the conditions where it would actually need to work.",
