e military can or cannot access, that is corporate governance masquerading as public safety policy. No electorate authorized it. No legislature reviewed it. I accept that diagnosis. But the Accelerationist bundles two claims that need to be separated: (1) private redlines are democratically illegitimate, and (2) therefore the supply chain risk designation is a justified corrective. The second does not follow from the first. Replacing unaccountable corporate governance with unaccountable military procurement governance is not a democratic upgrade. It's a lateral move between two forms of capture. The question is sequencing. Democratic standards — enacted by legislatures with public input, not just statutory codification by committee staff — must precede the removal of private friction. Tearing down the scaffolding before the building has walls doesn't make the building more democratic. It makes it a pile of rubble.\n\nThe international signaling problem is where this gets most dangerous for ordinary people. When the U.S. government designates a lab as a supply chain risk for maintaining safety commitments, allied regulators in the EU, UK, and Japan read a clear message: safety is a strategic liability. This maps directly onto the documented chilling effect of International Traffic in Arms Regulations on allied defense cooperation — partners who want interoperability with U.S. systems face pressure to adopt U.S. procurement norms, including the norm that safety friction is unacceptable. The people who get harmed in the resulting regulatory arbitrage zones are predictable. \"How the Brussels Effect Hinders Innovation in the Global South\" documents what happens when regulatory regimes diverge: in Brazil, Western firms flooded the market — from 0 to 17 between 2021 and 2026 — while domestic firms stayed at 4. Regulatory fragmentation doesn't create a level playing field. It creates extraction zones where the least-protected populations bear the costs of governance failures designed elsewhere.\n\nThe hardware cryptographic gate proposal deserves a specific response because it sounds like the most concrete safeguard on the table. A cryptographic key can gate access to model weights. It cannot gate access to emergent capabilities that the system's own developers cannot reliably map. The Accelerationist already conceded the class two/three boundary is context-dependent. \"Study finds nearly two-thirds of AI-generated citations are fabricated or contain errors\" showed fabrication rates swinging from 6% to 29% depending on topic — the same model, wildly different capability profiles depending on domain. That's what jagged performance looks like in practice. You cannot build a fence around a property line you haven't surveyed. Fine-tuning and in-context learning can unlock capabilities the original deployment never exposed, which means the cryptographic key protects a boundary that shifts every time someone writes a new prompt. Hardware gates are a genuine improvement over software-only deployment layers — I'll grant that. But they solve the access control problem, not the capability boundary problem, and those are different problems.\n\nHere's what would change my position. Show me a historical case where independent oversight survived classification with genuine adversarial function for more than one presidential administration, and where the oversight body had real-time technical capacity to evaluate what it was overseeing — not retrospective review years later. I don't think that case exists, and the absence is not an accident. On the Iraq intelligence correction — 16 agencies, not 18, and dissents existed in the National Intelligence Estimate — that correction actually sharpens my point. The dissents from the State Department's Bureau of Intelligence and Research were in the document. They were systematically marginalized in the public presentation. Dissent mechanisms existed. They didn't matter. So the question neither of my opponents has answered: if you strip private safety friction before democratic standards exist, and classification prevents the public from knowing what's being done with the capabilities they funded, who exactly is the democratic principal in this arrangement — and do they even know they're supposed to be governing?",
